r {} [:th {} "Format"] [:td {:colspan "2"} "Total of best lifts in military press, snatch, and clean &amp; jerk determined placement."]]]] [:div {:class "description"} [:p {} "There were seven entrants in this event, with German " [:a {:href "/athletes/55832"} "Rudolf Ismayr"] " slightly favored over Italy’s " [:a {:href "/athletes/56126"} "Carlo Galimberti"] ". Ismayr had held the world record in 1931 and was the 1931 European Champion. Galimberti was the defending silver medalist from " [:a {:href "/results/28716"} "Amsterdam"] ", and had also been runner-up at the Europeans in 1930-31. In 1930, that title went to " [:a {:href "/athletes/55827"} "Kurt Helbig"] ", who was the current world record holder and had won gold at " [:a {:href "/results/28702"} "Amsterdam in the lightweight class"] ", but he did not compete at Los Angeles. Another medal contender was Austrian " [:a {:href "/athletes/55196"} "Karl Hipfinger"] ", the 1929 European Champion."] "\n\n" [:p {} "Ismayr and Galimberti tied in the press with 102.5 kg, also equaled by " [:a {:href "/athletes/55664"} "Roger François"] " (FRA). Ismayr moved 5.0 kg ahead after the snatch, with Galimberti second, and François in third. The best lift in the clean &amp; jerk was by Hipfinger, which brought him the bronze medal. Ismayr remained in first to garner gold, with Galimberti taking another silver medal. In " [:a {:href "/results/28856"} "1936 at Berlin"] ", Ismayr would win the silver medal."] "\n"] [:table {:class "table table-striped"} [:thead {} [:tr {} [:th {} "Pos"] [:th {} "Competitor"] [:th {} "NOC"] [:th {} "Kilograms"] [:th {}] [:th {}] [:th {}]]] [:tbody {} [:tr {:class "", :style ""} [:td {} "1"] [:td {} [:a {:href "/athletes/55832"} "Rudolf Ismayr"]] [:td {} [:a {:href "/countries/GER"} [:img {:style "padding-right: 2px; vertical-align: middle", :src "https://olympedia-flags.s3.eu-central-1.amazonaws.com/GER.png"}] "GER"]] [:td {} "345.0"] [:td {} [:span {:class "Gold"} "Gold"]] [:td {} [:span {:class